3. Plans and machine licenses

Plans are described at https://orcraft.app/pricing/. A remote endpoint
machine is licensed to one account or team at a time, takes one slot when
a signed-in controller activates it, and gives the slot back when its
license is revoked. A machine license lasts a limited time and is renewed
by a signed-in controller. We may refuse or revoke activations that exceed
a plan or circumvent the counting. A controller running tests on its own
computer needs no account and no slot.

Machine licenses are renewed through the account service, so a lab
depends on the service being there. If we stop operating the account
service, we publish a final release of the Software whose machine
licenses do not expire and that needs no sign-in to keep running the
machines you hold, so that every lab keeps running without us. What ends
then is updates and support, not the Software you run. We tell account
holders by email before the service ends, and this promise binds anyone
we transfer Orcraft to.

4. Open source components

The Software includes open source libraries under their own licenses,
listed with the package. Nothing in this agreement limits the rights those
licenses give you in those libraries. The controller's user interface is
built with Qt through PySide6, used under the GNU Lesser General Public
License, version 3. Qt is installed beside the Software as a separate,
unmodified library, so you may replace it with your own build of the same
release.

5. Your tests and data

Your test suites, the systems under test, the results, logs and everything
else your tests produce are yours. The controller and the endpoint
application exchange them on your own network, and none of it reaches us.
We claim no rights over them. You are responsible for that content and for
the systems your tests act on.

